      "math_academy_id": "q-97187",
      "sequence_position": 13,
      "problem": "Given that $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.3)$ and $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.8)$ are independent, calculate $P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=1) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.3),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=0.3,\\,P(X=0)=0.7,\n$$\n\nand since $Y∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.8),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1)=0.8,\\,P(Y=0)=0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,\\cap \\,Y=1) & =P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.7\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
